'' 'private:' and 'protected:' inside a type, checked at the use site: four ways of
'' reaching one from outside, and the three contexts that may -- the type's own methods,
'' a derived type's, and a public member from anywhere. Then the IMPLICIT half: declaring
'' a type CALLS its default constructor and its destructor, and a private one is refused
'' with no member access written anywhere.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --dump-ir tests/diagnostics/src/member-visibility.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 1
'' GOLDEN: expected/member-visibility.out
